# Atlas — code owners for the ADMIN-OWNED governance surface.
#
# SCOPE IS DELIBERATELY NARROW. This file exists to gate the files that decide WHO MAY WRITE
# KNOWLEDGE — nothing else. A broad pattern here would put owner review on the critical path of
# every ordinary change, which is how a lock gets removed for being in the way.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO — read this before trusting it:
#   1. CODEOWNERS is INERT until branch protection sets `require_code_owner_reviews: true` on the
#      default branch. Merely committing this file gates nothing. See docs/governance/policy-lock.md.
#   2. It cannot distinguish two actors sharing ONE GitHub identity. If automation pushes with the
#      same credentials as the owner named below, this file does not constrain the automation — the
#      review requirement is satisfied by the identity, not by the human. Separating those identities
#      is the precondition that makes this lock mean what it says.
#   3. A team named here gates nothing unless that team has been granted access to THIS repository.
#      `@HuGR-Labs/atlas-admins` exists and contains the owner, but at the time of writing it holds
#      no repo grant — hence the individual owner is named alongside it, so the rule is live either
#      way. Removing the individual is safe ONLY after the team grant is confirmed.

# The governance policy itself: authz scopes (KNOW-11 owner-scoped write), the T0 heuristic, and the
# near-duplicate threshold. `.atlas/policy.json` names the actors authorized to write each scope;
# whoever can edit it can appoint themselves, which is precisely why it is gated here.
/.atlas/                      @HuGR-Labs/atlas-admins @gmhelmold

# The lock cannot be quietly removed by the thing it constrains.
/.github/CODEOWNERS           @HuGR-Labs/atlas-admins @gmhelmold

# The document that states what the lock is and how it is verified.
/docs/governance/             @HuGR-Labs/atlas-admins @gmhelmold
